From: portare-ch <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] libretro/vulkan: enable the device extensions the frontend requires

With the first context negotiation interface the frontend passes the
device extensions the core must enable, and cannot enable any itself on
a device the core creates. VkCreateDevice took the list and never read
it. RetroArch names VK_GOOGLE_display_timing there so it can time its
presents at a swap interval of 2; without it on the device, Flycast's
frames went through the repeated-present path instead.

Each extension the list names is enabled when the GPU has it and it is
not enabled already.
---
--- a/core/rend/vulkan/vk_context_lr.cpp
+++ b/core/rend/vulkan/vk_context_lr.cpp
@@ -24,6 +24,7 @@
 #include "oslib/oslib.h"
 #include "rend/transform_matrix.h"
 #include "texture.h"
+#include <cstring>
 #include <set>
 
 #if VULKAN_HPP_DISPATCH_LOADER_DYNAMIC == 1
@@ -184,6 +185,19 @@ bool VkCreateDevice(retro_vulkan_context* context, VkInstance instance, VkPhysic
 		VulkanContext::bufferDeviceAddressSupported = tryAddDeviceExtension(vk::KHRBufferDeviceAddressExtensionName);
 	}
 
+	// The libretro contract: the core enables what the frontend lists.
+	// RetroArch names the optional extensions it uses there as well, such
+	// as VK_GOOGLE_display_timing for timed presents, and cannot enable
+	// them on a device the core creates.
+	for (unsigned i = 0; i < num_required_device_extensions; i++)
+	{
+		bool enabled = false;
+		for (const char *name : enabledExtensions)
+			enabled = enabled || !strcmp(name, required_device_extensions[i]);
+		if (!enabled)
+			tryAddDeviceExtension(required_device_extensions[i]);
+	}
+
 	// Get device features
 
 	vk::StructureChain<
